            "messageBody": "\u2026rame\n\nThe product plan fixes image sampling at 5 FPS, requires the oldest\nunprocessed frame to be dropped when a new one arrives, and requires each\nframe to carry the session, a sequence number and a monotonic timestamp.\nNone of that existed: capture ran as fast as the encoder managed and the\nframes carried no metadata at all.\n\nSampling is now paced by CONFIG_BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_FPS (default 5).  Frames\narriving between samples overwrite the one before them, so what gets encoded\nis always the newest available frame, and the counters report both kinds of\nloss separately: sampler_skipped for frames the rate limiter passed on,\ndropped_oldest for unprocessed frames that a newer one replaced.\n\nagent_camera gained session=<id>, which prints the sequence and a\nCLOCK_MONOTONIC offset per frame -- the metadata an upload has to attach.\nMonotonic rather than wall clock on purpose: the first TLS handshake shoves\nthe realtime clock from 1970 to 2026 and would invalidate every timestamp\ntaken before it.\n\nThe hardware encoder's bitstream assembly is also two bugs less broken.  Its\ndrain DMA was stopped and re-armed per frame while the encoder kept running,\nwhich lost the bytes produced in between and reset the destination to the top\nof the buffer, so every frame began mid-bitstream; it now writes into a ring\nthat is never stopped for the whole session and a frame is a span of it.  And\nwrite_header() treated the block's own SOS as \"not a segment\" and stopped at\nit, reporting the entropy data as starting 16 bytes early -- the block does\nemit an SOS, contrary to the comment there.  Confirmed by re-assembling a\ncaptured frame on the host with the payload shifted by those 16 bytes: three\nframes of a static scene then agreed to within 2.8 grey levels, matching the\nsoftware encoder, while every other shift gave 3.6 or worse.\n\nThat is still not enough to trust it.  About one frame in three decodes with\nthe wrong DC and chroma -- a plausible picture with its brightness and colour\noff -- so CONFIG_BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_SW stays on and /dev/video0 keeps being\nserved by the software encoder at 3.7 fps.  Ruled out so far: quantisation\ntables (byte-identical across good and bad frames), frame-start alignment\n(every span already begins at SOI), sensor exposure and white balance (the\nUYVY path through the same sensor is stable over 14 frames), and a U/V swap\n(cross-channel correlation is negative).  Remaining clue: the bad frames'\ncontent is displaced vertically, best matching at 32 and 56 rows.\n\nAlso adds bk7258_dma_get_channel_dest_addr(), which the ring does *not* use:\nthe destination register turned out to hold the programmed start and not to\nadvance, measured as reading back the base address on six consecutive frames.\nIt is kept because that is worth knowing before someone tries it again.\n\nVerified on the board: 640x480 metadata 200ms apart, ring_over=0, audio\ncapture at 16kHz concurrent with capture reports underruns=0 ioerrors=0.",
