            "messageBody": "The plan is explicit that the panels must not run a real-time preview and\nmust refresh only when a key, a state or a result changes.  What the board\nactually did after boot was leave the greeting up, and the thing we reached\nfor when demonstrating the display was camera_preview -- a 30fps live stream,\ni.e. the one behaviour the plan rules out.\n\nbk7258_status_screen draws the state on fb0 and up to two lines of summary on\nfb1, and does nothing at all when what it would draw is already on the glass.\nThe state names are the plan's own (social IDLE/CONSENT/RECORDING/FINALIZING/\nRESULT_READY, voice LISTENING/TRANSCRIBING/THINKING/SPEAKING, plus CANCELLED\nand ERROR).  Bring-up leaves the board in IDLE, so the greeting is no longer\nthe last thing on screen.\n\nOne line is printed per actual repaint.  That is deliberate: there is no way\nto read the glass back, so it is the only outside evidence the panel was\npainted, and it makes the \"only on change\" rule checkable -- a screen that\nrepainted on a timer would show up as a stream.  Boot prints it exactly once.\n\nChinese summaries cannot be drawn yet: the stroke font covers ASCII only, so\nbk7258_status_screen_summary() returns -ENOSYS for non-ASCII rather than\ndrawing blanks and pretending.  A caller that gets -ENOSYS can fall back to\nTTS, which the plan makes the primary output channel anyway.  Wiring in a CJK\nglyph source is separate work.\n\ncamera_preview stays as a diagnostic -- it is how a byte-order or geometry\nfault is made visible -- it just is not what the product path draws.",
