            "messageBody": "Settings that must not be committed -- the agent's API key and base URL, the\nWLAN credentials -- had nowhere to live, so they were being typed in again\nafter every reset or, worse, pasted into a source file.\n\nThis adds a small key/value store with an nsh front end:\n\n  kvdb set llm.key sk-...      kvdb get llm.host      kvdb list\n\nValues whose key ends in .key or .psk are masked on the way out unless the\ncaller asks for the raw value, so `kvdb list` can be pasted into a bug report.\nBring-up injects the stored LLM settings into the agent's config file, so\nnothing has to be re-entered per app.\n\nThe store is in memory for now and says so: `kvdb set` answers \"stored for\nthis boot only (flash backend disabled)\" and bk7258_kvdb_persistent() returns\nfalse, which callers surface to the user.  CONFIG_BK7258_KVDB_FLASH turns on\nthe flash backend and is off by default because it does not work yet -- see\nbelow.\n\nTalking to the CP's flash server needs three things this port did not do, all\nfound on hardware: the payload needs a CRC-8/0x31, the descriptor has to live\nin the SWAP window rather than in AP RAM, and the ack has to report\nACK_STATE_COMPLETE or the server's socket stays in \"receive in process\" and\nrejects everything after the first request.  With those, the connect succeeds:\n\"flash: connected to the CP flash server (cpu0 port1)\".\n\nThe read does not.  It is issued, the link goes down 190ms later, and\nbk7258_flash_read() never returns:\n\n  IPC:E: ipc_router_tx_cmpl_isr tx2 error @440! 150 != 0.\n  ap_bridg:W: link down event=a2\n\nThe CP casts the ack box straight to its command struct and checks that param1\nstill matches what it queued (cp/.../mb_ipc.c:432); it queued 0x150 and read\nback 0, so it drops the frame without dequeuing and the router takes the link\ndown.  Echoing param1 in the ack was tried and changes nothing, so the ack the\nCP reads is filled somewhere below our ACK_BOX frame.  Next step is to find\nwhat physically fills ack_buf, in the CP's mb_chnl driver.\n\nTwo things here are worth keeping regardless of that.\n\nThe load runs from a task, not from bring-up.  A cross-core request blocks,\nand blocking bring-up stops the mailbox from being serviced, so the heartbeat\nlapses and the CP's 8-second watchdog resets the chip -- a reboot loop\nprinting \"connected to the CP flash server\" every ~9 seconds.  From a task, a\nmissing flash service only delays that task.\n\nPointers from the reply are validated before use.  Both the descriptor (the\nframe's reserved field) and the read payload pointer inside it are addresses\nthe other core wrote; flash_peer_ptr_ok() accepts only CP RAM and the SWAP\nwindow and returns -EFAULT otherwise, instead of faulting on a bad one -- an\nAP fault is nearly invisible here, since arm_lowputc() goes out over the same\nmailbox.\n\nWorth recording for whoever picks this up: the AP console is a mailbox tunnel,\nso once the link is down no printf from the AP comes out.  Two rounds of\ndiagnostics were wasted learning that.  The CP's log and its sources are the\nonly evidence that survives the failure.",
