            "messageBody": "\u2026path in use\n\nThe camera document had grown two correction banners, a seven-row comparison\nof the two encoder paths, and a full narrative of a defect investigation --\n216 lines to answer \"how do I grab a frame and what are the panel\nparameters\".  It is now 143 and leads with the interface; the root-cause\ndetail lives in the code comments and the plan note, where it is useful.\n\nThe images and the clip were shot on a path that is no longer the default and\nat a rate the text no longer claimed, so they have been retaken: the frame and\nthe 8-frame clip both come from the software encoder at its measured 3.7 fps.\nThe clip is renamed clip_640x480.mp4 -- without the rate in the filename,\nsince the rate changes whenever the encoder does.\n\nTwo verification lessons are recorded there, both learned the hard way in this\nwork:\n\n  Judge assembly by spatial smoothness and the absence of a seam, not by the\n  mean difference between frames.  Mean difference is dominated by global\n  brightness; an exposure change alone moves it by tens of grey levels.\n\n  Check chroma.  Every check here ran on the luma plane, which is blind to\n  the fault that actually shipped: those frames passed on smoothness with no\n  seam while the colour error was plainly visible.  Per-frame luma, saturated\n  pixel share, mean Cr and R-B catches it immediately.\n\n  Take enough frames.  Three frames hid this defect completely; twelve\n  exposed it reliably.\n\n.gitignore stops tracking .superpowers/, which is local workspace state.",
